The last step of the proof of Theorem 2.2.7 is invalid for non-projective $X$. Having shown $T_{\mathbb{Q}}(X) := NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)^{\perp} \subset W_{\mathbb{Q}}$, the proof concludes $p(T_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)) = p(H^2(X,\mathbb{Q}))$ 'because the kernel of $p$ is $NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)$'. That inference needs $H^2(X,\mathbb{Q}) = NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X) + T_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)$, i.e. $q|_{NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)}$ non-degenerate. For projective $X$ this holds (an ample class gives signature $(1,ho-1)$). But when $NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X) \subset \eta^{\perp}$ - precisely the non-projective situation the paper isolates in Lemma 4.4.1 and Corollary 4.4.3 - the class $\eta$ is isotropic and orthogonal to all of $NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X)$, so it lies in the radical, $NS_{\mathbb{Q}} \cap T_{\mathbb{Q}} 
eq 0$, and $\dim_{\mathbb{Q}} p(T_{\mathbb{Q}}) < \dim_{\mathbb{Q}} p(H^2(X,\mathbb{Q}))$. The conclusion itself then fails too: since $W_{\mathbb{Q}} = \eta^{\perp} \cap H^2(X,\mathbb{Q})$ is a hyperplane and $\ker p = NS_{\mathbb{Q}}$, one has $p(W_{\mathbb{Q}}) = p(H^2(X,\mathbb{Q}))$ if and only if $NS_{\mathbb{Q}}(X) 
ot\subset \eta^{\perp}$; otherwise the two differ in codimension one and $\Sha^0$ is isogenous to $H^{0,2}(X)/p(W_{\mathbb{Z}})$, not to $H^{0,2}(X)/p(H^2(X,\mathbb{Z}))$. Severity minor: the one downstream consumer, Corollary 2.2.8, survives - run the density argument with $W$ in place of $H^2$, which works because $\sigma,  ar\sigma \in W_{\mathbb{C}}$ forces $p(W_{\mathbb{R}}) = H^{0,2}(X)$ - and the places that invoke Theorem 2.2.7 reduce to projective $X$ first. The fix is to add a projectivity (or $NS_{\mathbb{Q}} 
ot\subset \eta^{\perp}$) hypothesis, or to state the theorem with $p(W_{\mathbb{Z}})$.
